1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,181
ROSIE:

2
00:00:00,181 --> 00:00:04,750
Colin, I’m really struggling to think of a topic for our human geography

3
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:04,800
assignment.

4
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:05,031
COLIN:

5
00:00:05,031 --> 00:00:05,854
Me too, Rosie.

6
00:00:05,854 --> 00:00:09,981
I’ll tell you what, let’s think about the different aspects of human

7
00:00:09,981 --> 00:00:10,271
geography,

8
00:00:10,271 --> 00:00:14,339
and see if we can narrow the topic down a bit to help us

9
00:00:14,339 --> 00:00:14,630
decide.

10
00:00:14,630 --> 00:00:14,680
ROSIE:

11
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:15,356
OK.

12
00:00:15,356 --> 00:00:17,170
So one aspect is population.

13
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:21,524
That would be all about population density and migration and so on.

14
00:00:21,524 --> 00:00:24,790
Lots of facts and statistics … Maybe a bit boring?

15
00:00:24,790 --> 00:00:25,157
COLIN:

16
00:00:25,157 --> 00:00:25,524
Yeah,

17
00:00:25,524 --> 00:00:28,460
but quite straightforward to find on the internet.

18
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:28,785
ROSIE:

19
00:00:28,785 --> 00:00:29,435
Suppose so.

20
00:00:29,435 --> 00:00:30,411
How about health?

21
00:00:30,411 --> 00:00:35,287
I’d never thought about the links between that and geography until Professor Lee gave

22
00:00:35,287 --> 00:00:36,913
us that lecture on cholera.

23
00:00:36,913 --> 00:00:40,164
How in the nineteenth century a physician used street plans

24
00:00:40,164 --> 00:00:44,390
and plans of water supplies to find the source of a cholera epidemic.

25
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:44,847
COLIN:

26
00:00:44,847 --> 00:00:47,130
Yes – fascinating, wasn’t it?

27
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:51,240
Or we could do something more general – like economies.

28
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:51,525
ROSIE:

29
00:00:51,525 --> 00:00:54,950
So how financial and commercial factors are linked to the physical environment?

30
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:55,425
COLIN:

31
00:00:55,425 --> 00:00:55,900
Yeah.

32
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:56,088
ROSIE:

33
00:00:56,088 --> 00:00:58,350
I thought that had been taken off the syllabus for this year.

34
00:00:58,350 --> 00:00:58,637
COLIN:

35
00:00:58,637 --> 00:00:59,210
Has it?

36
00:00:59,210 --> 00:00:59,406
ROSIE:

37
00:00:59,406 --> 00:01:00,191
I’m not sure.

38
00:01:00,191 --> 00:01:01,760
But it might be best to avoid it.

39
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:02,201
COLIN:

40
00:01:02,201 --> 00:01:02,641
OK.

41
00:01:02,641 --> 00:01:05,725
Maybe we could do something on culture.

42
00:01:05,725 --> 00:01:10,130
We had that lecture about culture and geography last week.

43
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:10,383
ROSIE:

44
00:01:10,383 --> 00:01:12,407
I didn’t get much out of that.

45
00:01:12,407 --> 00:01:15,950
It was all so general and the lecturer didn’t give any useful examples.

46
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:16,242
COLIN:

47
00:01:16,242 --> 00:01:19,740
Yeah, I hardly took any notes, it didn’t seem worth it.

48
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:20,130
ROSIE:

49
00:01:20,130 --> 00:01:20,910
Me neither.

50
00:01:20,910 --> 00:01:21,224
COLIN:

51
00:01:21,224 --> 00:01:22,791
We could focus on poverty.

52
00:01:22,791 --> 00:01:25,300
That’s something that’s a global problem …

53
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:25,595
ROSIE:

54
00:01:25,595 --> 00:01:27,659
The trouble is, Dr Lee was saying,

55
00:01:27,659 --> 00:01:31,786
that you have to be careful with some of the figures relating to poverty

56
00:01:31,786 --> 00:01:33,260
– they’re sometimes deliberately manipulated.

57
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:33,641
COLIN:

58
00:01:33,641 --> 00:01:37,070
You mean the information gets changed for political reasons?

59
00:01:37,070 --> 00:01:37,353
ROSIE:

60
00:01:37,353 --> 00:01:38,770
That sort of thing, yes.

61
00:01:38,770 --> 00:01:39,076
ROSIE:

62
00:01:39,076 --> 00:01:42,140
So what are we going to do our assignment on?

63
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:46,429
I’ll tell you what, one of the possibilities we haven’t discussed is

64
00:01:46,429 --> 00:01:46,735
urbanisation.

65
00:01:46,735 --> 00:01:50,105
And now over half the world’s population lives in cities,

66
00:01:50,105 --> 00:01:51,330
that’s really important.

67
00:01:51,330 --> 00:01:51,650
COLIN:

68
00:01:51,650 --> 00:01:52,930
OK, good idea, Rosie.

69
00:01:52,930 --> 00:01:54,210
Let’s do that.

70
00:01:54,210 --> 00:01:54,448
ROSIE:

71
00:01:54,448 --> 00:01:56,113
I love living in a big city,

72
00:01:56,113 --> 00:01:57,540
but of course there are problems.

73
00:01:57,540 --> 00:01:57,912
COLIN:

74
00:01:57,912 --> 00:01:59,770
Things like theft and robbery?

75
00:01:59,770 --> 00:02:00,034
ROSIE:

76
00:02:00,034 --> 00:02:00,298
Yes,

77
00:02:00,298 --> 00:02:01,618
but where I come from,

78
00:02:01,618 --> 00:02:03,730
that’s linked to another more serious issue,

79
00:02:03,730 --> 00:02:06,634
which is that a lot of people don’t have jobs.

80
00:02:06,634 --> 00:02:08,218
That’s getting worse and worse.

81
00:02:08,218 --> 00:02:12,970
And we also still have quite a lot of people who are homeless, though that’s not quite

82
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:14,290
so bad as it was.

83
00:02:14,290 --> 00:02:14,622
COLIN:

84
00:02:14,622 --> 00:02:17,940
It’d be nice to talk about some positive developments.

85
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:21,590
Like some of the new developments on the outskirts of cities.

86
00:02:21,590 --> 00:02:21,887
ROSIE:

87
00:02:21,887 --> 00:02:22,184
Yeah.

88
00:02:22,184 --> 00:02:25,450
They’ve opened some massive new shopping centres outside my city.

89
00:02:25,450 --> 00:02:25,751
COLIN:

90
00:02:25,751 --> 00:02:29,966
Yeah, the same with mine – but it’s meant a lot of the shops

91
00:02:29,966 --> 00:02:32,074
in the city centre are closing down.

92
00:02:32,074 --> 00:02:36,289
But the outskirts are ideal for buildings that need a lot of space, like

93
00:02:36,289 --> 00:02:36,891
for conferences.

94
00:02:36,891 --> 00:02:39,300
They’ve opened a couple of big ones.

95
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:39,584
ROSIE:

96
00:02:39,584 --> 00:02:41,290
Yes, we’ve got some too.

97
00:02:41,290 --> 00:02:44,986
It’d be nicer to have more facilities for things like football too.

98
00:02:44,986 --> 00:02:47,260
But that’s not happening where I live.

99
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:47,576
COLIN:

100
00:02:47,576 --> 00:02:48,840
Same in my area.

101
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:49,151
ROSIE:

102
00:02:49,151 --> 00:02:51,951
We could include something about developing disused industrial sites.

103
00:02:51,951 --> 00:02:53,818
It seems like a good idea,

104
00:02:53,818 --> 00:02:57,240
because you’re not doing any harm to the natural environment.

105
00:02:57,240 --> 00:02:57,727
COLIN:

106
00:02:57,727 --> 00:02:58,213
Yeah,

107
00:02:58,213 --> 00:03:01,620
but aren’t the buildings architecturally significant?

108
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:01,944
ROSIE:

109
00:03:01,944 --> 00:03:02,591
Not really.

110
00:03:02,591 --> 00:03:06,801
And what people forget is that they often used quite dangerous materials – chemicals

111
00:03:06,801 --> 00:03:09,716
and things – which haven’t been properly cleared away,

112
00:03:09,716 --> 00:03:12,630
so the whole site has to be made safe.

113
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:12,883
COLIN:

114
00:03:12,883 --> 00:03:13,136
Mm.

115
00:03:13,136 --> 00:03:14,402
That can’t be cheap.

116
00:03:14,402 --> 00:03:16,680
And I bet it’s often not budgeted for.

117
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:16,958
ROSIE:

118
00:03:16,958 --> 00:03:17,790
You’re right.

119
00:03:17,790 --> 00:03:18,101
COLIN:

120
00:03:18,101 --> 00:03:19,970
Have you read about Masdar City?

121
00:03:19,970 --> 00:03:20,266
ROSIE:

122
00:03:20,266 --> 00:03:21,154
In Abu Dhabi?

123
00:03:21,154 --> 00:03:24,704
Yes, it was designed to be a green city, wasn’t it?

124
00:03:24,704 --> 00:03:28,846
That might be a good example of a city which set out to depend

125
00:03:28,846 --> 00:03:30,030
entirely on renewable energy.

126
00:03:30,030 --> 00:03:30,436
COLIN:

127
00:03:30,436 --> 00:03:33,279
Yes, we should say something about that.

128
00:03:33,279 --> 00:03:38,964
It was designed to be totally pedestrianised too, wasn’t it, with the transport

129
00:03:38,964 --> 00:03:39,370
underground.

130
00:03:39,370 --> 00:03:39,689
ROSIE:

131
00:03:39,689 --> 00:03:40,008
Yes,

132
00:03:40,008 --> 00:03:44,478
and they had big plans for recycling to reduce waste to the lowest possible

133
00:03:44,478 --> 00:03:44,797
level.

134
00:03:44,797 --> 00:03:47,670
But let’s stick to talking about power sources.

135
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:48,040
COLIN:

136
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:51,366
Then there’s that ecotown in England, Greenhill Abbots.

137
00:03:51,366 --> 00:03:56,171
It set out to conform to the usual principles – sustainability and so on.

138
00:03:56,171 --> 00:03:59,498
A lot of people were against it at first;

139
00:03:59,498 --> 00:04:01,715
they said the plans were unrealistic.

140
00:04:01,715 --> 00:04:06,890
I’m not sure how far they’ve got with it – I’ll check.

141
00:04:06,890 --> 00:04:07,520
ROSIE:

142
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:08,150
Right.

143
00:04:08,150 --> 00:04:10,670
So it looks as …
